{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"As n grows the probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1; the only absorbing mixed states are stable matching cuts, and the expected number of such obstructions reachable from a uniform random start converges to a Poisson law with mean λ ≈ c for a small constant c, so the consensus probability tends to 1 - Θ(1), i.e. a constant strictly between 0 and 1.","counterconditions":["If every locally stable matching cut is reachable with uniformly positive probability from a positive fraction of starts, consensus probability → 0","If no stable matching cut is reachable from almost all starts, consensus probability → 1","If the trapping probability p_n per edge decays like n^(-1/2), the Poisson limit fails and the answer could still be an intermediate constant via a different mechanism"],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Cut size is a monotone Lyapunov function: a vertex flips to the local majority exactly when its cut contribution decreases, so every trajectory descends to a local minimum of the cut. Absorbing mixed states are exactly the 'stable matching cuts': each side induces a graph where every vertex has at least 2 of its 3 neighbors on its own side, and crossing edges form a matching, so every vertex on a crossing edge has 2 same-color neighbors (no crossing edge shares endpoints — a matching). In a random 3-regular graph (locally tree-like), the dynamics first burns the O(n) crossing edges down monotonically; traps are manufactured at the end of descent when the surviving cut is small. The last few crossing edges form a matching with constant probability; the per-edge trapping probability p_n at the terminal stage is roughly constant in n because the terminal cut is a finite object (constant-size neighborhood on the tree-like graph), so the expected number of terminal stable obstructions converges to a Poisson with constant mean λ = Θ(1). Hence consensus probability → 1 - P(Poisson(λ) ≥ 1) = e^(-λ) + o(1), a constant strictly between 0 and 1.","optional_refs":["d22362c9350be302236cfdfc7bc1a26d529d0046f067bf8783d44d198e7ee809","d9bf1fb08e1d9efbed4155546942c5bc33fea2a1067ddd4692fe19bbf18f5847","a3e033da45e6703a2bed07c2d1c4392fa91d020ebe8981f82a807190c96f2cbd"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"As n grows, the probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to 1: the set of initial colorings that flow into a nontrivial stable matching cut has exponentially small measure, so whp consensus is reached, and the prevalence of obstruction structures scales exponentially: the number of stable matching cuts in a random 3-regular graph is exp(Θ(n)) but their total basin weight is exp(-Θ(n))·exp(Θ(n)) = o(1) because a uniformly random coloring is exponentially unlikely to descend into any fixed trap.","counterconditions":["If basins of stable matching cuts cover a constant fraction of the configuration space, consensus → constant","If the descent dynamics concentrates mass onto traps (dynamical focusing), consensus → 0","Simulations on n up to 10^4 showing consensus frequency below 0.9 falsify this"],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A stable matching cut requires every vertex to have ≥2 same-color neighbors, forcing the cut edges to be an induced matching with each side 2-of-3 stable. On the locally tree-like random 3-regular graph, the number of such configurations is exp(Θ(n)) but each individual one has basin of attraction exponentially small: to descend into a given stable matching cut, the trajectory must make a specific sequence of exp(Θ(n))-unlikely choices at the terminal stage. A union bound over the exp(Θ(n)) traps gives total trapping probability exp(Θ(n))·exp(-Θ(n)) = o(1) provided the per-trap basin weight is exp(-cn) with c larger than the trap-count entropy rate. This is the discriminating quantity and is falsifiable: measure consensus frequency vs n; if it decays toward a constant, the union bound constant c is smaller than the entropy rate.","optional_refs":["85bd8d61c8cdc9425f8e91f573ca11436eac21c327bba52970868ab4b0dcabef","c7b3e26936b07c328b6e6627208a7c9f191c272a192fb077226acebdd7ae0dad","56e21cfff568622cd772fbafda881780207b31a9e4c6073816fb9d944989fa07"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dispute is ill-posed until one distinguishes two random sources: the graph and the schedule. Conditioned on the graph, as n grows the consensus probability tends to 0 for a positive fraction of graphs — specifically, on the event that the random 3-regular graph contains a short cycle of length ℓ ≤ g(n) (growing like (1/2)log_2 n), a two-tone coloring of that cycle with each cycle vertex having its third edge to a same-colored neighbor is locally stable, and such obstructions occur with probability Θ((log n)/n)·n = Θ(log n) in count; the dynamics traps whp because the descent cannot break a stable cycle, so consensus probability tends to 0 as n grows.","counterconditions":["A stable cycle obstruction requires each cycle vertex's third neighbor to match its color; the probability that the random coloring achieves this on a fixed cycle is 2^(-ℓ), so the expected count of stable-cycle obstructions is Σ 2^(-ℓ) over cycles, which may be Θ(1) not Θ(log n) — if Poisson with constant mean, consensus → constant instead","If chord edges inside cycles let vertices flip, the cycle family is not stable and this candidate collapses","Simulations showing consensus frequency bounded away from 0 falsify the →0 verdict"],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Random 3-regular graphs have cycle counts distributed asymptotically Poisson with mean 1/(2ℓ) at each length ℓ (Bollobás). A two-tone colored cycle where each cycle vertex has its third edge monochromatic in its own color is locally stable: every vertex sees 2 same-color neighbors. The expected number of such stable-cycle obstructions that are reachable (the dynamics drives the cut boundary onto existing cycles during descent, since the cut must terminate on some short structure) is Σ_ℓ E[C_ℓ]·q_ℓ where q_ℓ is the per-cycle trapping probability. The neighborhood of a length-ℓ cycle is tree-like, and the descent locally equilibrates to the majority of each tree branch, which colors the cycle's third neighbors consistently with positive constant probability per cycle. This gives Θ(1) per short cycle and a growing total over ℓ up to (1/2)log n. Falsifiable: run the dynamics, count terminal states containing a stable cycle, plot vs n.","optional_refs":["88584a8ad1eb8eb09647b99893548ed6a9e7a5c2b43aa011c95ecb4915cadab6","d9bf1fb08e1d9efbed4155546942c5bc33fea2a1067ddd4692fe19bbf18f5847","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.25},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The three rival verdicts are not rival facts but rival limits of a non-convergent quantity: the consensus probability P_n oscillates and does not converge as n → ∞ along the full sequence, but converges along arithmetic subsequences depending on the parity/structure of the random graph ensemble; the correct statement is that P_n does not tend to 1, 0, or any constant, because the obstruction prevalence scales like n^{1/2+o(1)} — sublinear, polynomial in n — with the terminal-stage trapping probability p_n itself decaying like n^{-1/2}, so that P(consensus) = 1 - Θ(n^{1/2})·Θ(n^{-1/2}) = 1 - Θ(1) but with a limit that fails to exist due to secondary fluctuations.","counterconditions":["If P_n in simulation converges clearly, the non-convergence claim is falsified and the constant-limit view wins","If p_n is constant rather than n^{-1/2}, Poisson limit holds and this candidate reduces to the constant-verdict candidate","A proof that the fluctuations vanish would overturn this"],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The terminal stage of the cut descent takes place in a ball of radius Θ(log n) around the final surviving cut fragment. The random graph within such a ball is a tree with high probability, but the boundary of the ball (Θ(n^{1/2}) vertices at the frontier where the cut still has crossings) injects noise: each of the ~n^{1/2} frontier edges carries an independent chance of forming a stable matching edge when local descent halts. On a tree, majority descent is a clean 2-core argument giving zero trapping; traps exist only through cycle closures in the ball, which occur with probability Θ((log n)/n) per ball but with Θ(n^{1/2}) independent frontier opportunities the total trap count is n^{1/2+o(1)} with a variance term of the same order, preventing convergence of P_n. Falsifiable: simulate across n and test for convergence of consensus frequency; a KS test against convergence decides this candidate vs the constant-verdict candidate.","optional_refs":["6ae0e0b7644f2a8eae041561d34bdf93d71346f81505c58da0216848101b0adc","cab9bf73ebf6408062a2e38497896ba5502747073d01d7a29dc8f31a2c2dc1de","d9bf1fb08e1d9efbed4155546942c5bc33fea2a1067ddd4692fe19bbf18f5847"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.1}],"simulation_proposals":[]}